# =============================================================================
# .env.example — every setting google-maps-harness reads, with the defaults it
# uses when you leave one out.
#
# Copy this to a file only you can read, fill in the key, and name that file on
# the command line:
#
#   cp .env.example .env && chmod 600 .env
#   claude mcp add google-maps -- /abs/path/.venv/bin/google-maps-harness \
#       --env-file /abs/path/.env
#
# SECURITY: the key belongs in this file rather than on the registration
# command. A value passed with `-e NAME=value` is written into ~/.claude.json
# and into your shell history, and neither of those is owner-only. Nothing is
# loaded unless --env-file names it: this server never picks up a .env it found
# lying in a working directory.
#
# A test holds this file and config.SETTING_NAMES in step, so a variable that
# appears in one and not the other fails the suite.
# =============================================================================

# --- Required -----------------------------------------------------------------

# Your Google Maps Platform API key.
#
# Create it at https://console.cloud.google.com/google/maps-apis/credentials and
# restrict it — under "API restrictions" — to exactly the APIs you want this
# server to reach:
#
#   Geocoding API            geocode_address, reverse_geocode, geocode_place_id
#   Places API (New)         search_places_by_text, search_places_nearby,
#                            get_place_details, autocomplete_places
#   Routes API               compute_route, compute_route_matrix
#   Time Zone API            get_time_zone
#   Elevation API            get_elevation
#   Address Validation API   validate_address
#   Air Quality API          get_air_quality
#
# A tool whose API you left disabled refuses with a message saying so, which is
# a fine way to run a smaller surface deliberately.
#
# Set a daily quota cap on each API in the console too. Google enforces that at
# its own edge, which is the only place a spend cap holds no matter what runs on
# this machine.
GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY=

# --- Optional -----------------------------------------------------------------

# Connect and read timeout for one request to Google, in seconds. 1 to 60.
# GOOGLE_MAPS_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=10

# How many upstream requests one tool call may make. 1 to 200. This is what
# bounds the product of the loops inside a call: a paged search whose every
# result is then looked up in detail is more requests than any single loop's
# limit describes.
# GOOGLE_MAPS_MAX_REQUESTS_PER_CALL=25

# How long one tool call may spend on upstream requests, in seconds. 5 to 300.
# GOOGLE_MAPS_MAX_SECONDS_PER_CALL=30

# Two-letter CLDR region code used to break ties on ambiguous place names —
# "Springfield" and "Boston" exist in several countries. Unset by default, which
# leaves the decision to Google.
# GOOGLE_MAPS_REGION_CODE=US

# Language for place names, address formatting, and route instructions.
# GOOGLE_MAPS_LANGUAGE_CODE=en

# Whether place lookups may request the "atmosphere" detail tier: reviews,
# editorial summaries, and the service attributes alongside them.
#
# Off by default for two reasons that point the same way. It is Google's most
# expensive Places tier, and it is the tier that carries prose members of the
# public wrote — which lands in the model's context and is exactly where a
# prompt injection would arrive. Turn it on when you want an agent reading
# reviews, and leave it off when you do not.
#
# The other three tiers stay available either way: "enterprise" already carries
# ratings, opening hours, phone numbers, and websites.
# GOOGLE_MAPS_ALLOW_ATMOSPHERE_FIELDS=false
